The baseline already uses the minimum of one ciphertext multiplication. The circuit must differ from the baseline to count, so I'll try `square(x)`. It is counted as a ciphertext-ciphertext multiplication but takes a cheaper path in SEAL: it needs fewer NTT operations than a general multiply. Its operation counts differ from the baseline's (square=1 instead of ct_ct_mul=1), so it qualifies as a distinct circuit.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext y = square(x);
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The baseline already needs only one ciphertext multiplication, which is the minimum for x². Without numeric constants I can't scale or halve a rewritten form, so no cheaper algebraic form exists. The `square` variant was no faster either.

For this attempt I'll build x² from `(-x) * x`, negated back. It has a different operation count (extra negations), but I don't expect it to beat the baseline. It is mainly a probe of whether the multiplication path matters.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext nx = -x;
  Ciphertext y = -(nx * x);
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The multiplication is the floor here: x² needs one ciphertext multiplication, and any rewrite adds work. The negation variant was within noise of the baseline (0.99x, faster in 1 of 5 batches).

I'll try one more variant: `(x + x) * x` is 2x², so I can't use it directly. Instead I'll go back to the plain product with no extra operations. It is the same circuit as the baseline and can't win, but I have no cheaper form to offer.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext y = x * x;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I don't have a cheaper circuit. x² needs exactly one ciphertext multiplication with its relinearization and rescale, and no numeric constants or extra plaintext inputs are available to build a cheaper identity. Rotations, plaintext multiplications and alignments would only add cost.

The remaining option is a structural variant with the same multiplication count. It negates only one operand, so it uses one fewer negation than attempt 2. It is expected to land within noise of the baseline.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext y = -(x * (-x));
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

There is still no cheaper circuit than one ciphertext multiplication. I'll try one last structural variant that keeps a single multiply but adds an explicit `x - x` zero term, to see how CHEHAB normalizes it. It can't be faster than the baseline in principle, so this is only a probe.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext z = x - x;
  Ciphertext y = x * x + z;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I don't have a cheaper circuit to offer. The baseline's single multiply (with its relinearize and rescale) is already the minimum for x². CHEHAB also removes the redundant terms I added, so those variants collapse to the baseline's circuit. I'm submitting the baseline form again rather than a variant that would only add cost.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext y = x * x;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I have no cheaper circuit. Every attempt so far either reproduced the baseline's circuit or added operations. One ciphertext multiplication is the floor for x², and with no constants or extra plaintext inputs there is nothing to trade it for. I'm keeping the baseline as the best and submitting it unchanged.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext y = x * x;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I still have no cheaper circuit. The baseline is already at the minimum of one ciphertext multiplication, and everything I tried either collapsed to the same circuit or added operations.

For the last attempt I'll go back to the `square(x)` form from attempt 1. It was a distinct circuit that measured 67.70 ms against 65.95 ms in a single timing, which is within run-to-run noise but was not confirmed faster.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext y = square(x);
  y.set_output("y");
}
```
